.TH COMM 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
comm \- print lines common to two files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B comm
.RB [ \-123 ]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Comm
reads file1 and file2, which should be in sort, and produces a
three-column output: lines only in file1; lines only in file2;
and lines in both files.
.PP
Flags 1, 2, or 3 suppress printing of the corresponding column.
Thus
.B comm \-12
prints only the lines common to the two files;
.B comm \-23
prints only lines in the first file but not in the second;
.B comm \-123
prints nothing.
Remaining columns are separated by tabs.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/COMM  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sort(1), cmp(1), diff(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 2 for input-open or usage errors.
.SH BUGS
Both operands must be file names; '-' is not a standard-input alias. Sorted order is assumed, not checked.
Lines longer than 255 bytes are treated as several logical lines.
